<section class="blk">
<h2>Identified, not yet consulted</h2>
  <h3>Argentina and Uruguay</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Civil registration, <strong>Santa Fe</strong> province (Rosario), from the 1880s.</li>
    <li>Catholic parish registers, <strong>Archdiocese of Rosario</strong>, via FamilySearch.</li>
    <li><strong>CEMLA</strong> immigrant database and the <em>listas de pasajeros</em> into Buenos Aires.</li>
    <li><strong>Boletín Oficial de la República Argentina</strong> — consular appointments.</li>
    <li>Argentine <strong>Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores</strong> <em>Memorias</em> and diplomatic lists.</li>
    <li><strong>Argentine Stud Book</strong> — Lerena entries as owner and breeder; Haras El Viejo.</li>
    <li>Uruguayan civil and parish registers, <strong>Montevideo</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>South Africa</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NAAIRS</strong> — deceased estate files, wills, departmental records. Highest-value target.</li>
    <li>Civil registration; Department of Home Affairs and the provincial archives.</li>
    <li><strong>Union of South Africa Government Gazette</strong> and Cape Colony gazettes — the
      <em>exequatur</em> recognising a foreign consul.</li>
    <li><strong>South African Stud Book</strong> and Jockey Club registers, including imports.</li>
    <li><strong>C.P. Nel Museum</strong>, Oudtshoorn, and the Cape archives — the ostrich feather trade.</li>
    <li>Cape and Rand newspapers.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Spain</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>INE</strong> provincial surname distribution — to test La Rioja against Badajoz.</li>
    <li>Parish and civil registers in whichever province the emigration record eventually names.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>The family's own records</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>David Defranceski's <strong>MyHeritage tree</strong>, with its stored facts, citations and media.
      Not yet read into this archive.</li>
    <li>Australian records for the Johannesburg-to-Brisbane migration, subject to the rule on living people.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

